                <p>本周值得关注的，不是某个广告渠道突然放量，而是跨境电商独立站团队正在重新审视预算：<strong>广告带来的订单，究竟有没有留下利润？</strong></p>
<p>过去常见的做法是按“ prospecting（ prospecting获客）—retargeting（再营销）”或渠道比例分预算。但当平台转化回传延迟、退款增加、物流成本波动时，这种分配方式很容易失真。近期我们从广告开户、充值、多账户管理和财务对账场景中发现，团队的核心问题已经从“预算够不够”变成“预算应该投向哪里、投多少、何时停”。</p>
<h2>变化一：预算表正在增加毛利，而不只显示ROAS</h2>
<p>不少运营仍习惯用ROAS判断渠道效率：花费100元带来300元销售额，便继续加预算。但对独立站而言，这一算法过于粗略。</p>
<p>更实用的计算方式是：</p>
<blockquote>
<p>贡献毛利 = 实收收入－商品成本－履约物流费－支付费用－退款损失－广告花费</p>
</blockquote>
<p>某工具类 App团队曾发现，某渠道ROAS稳定，但退款率上升后，贡献利润连续下滑。团队暂停了按单日ROAS自动扩量，改为按SKU核算净利润，并重新测试落地页中的价格承诺。这个调整比单纯降低CPC更有效，因为问题来自流量质量，而不是流量价格。</p>
<p>需要提醒的是，平台归因收入与独立站实收金额通常不会完全一致。预算复盘至少要区分“平台归因订单”“已支付订单”和“扣除退款后的净收入”，不要把三个口径混在同一张报表里。</p>
<h2>变化二：预算增减必须和素材疲劳一起判断</h2>
<p>本周另一个明显趋势，是成熟账户的获客成本压力增大，团队开始重新安排测试预算。</p>
<p>比较稳妥的月度分配方式是：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>60%—70%：</strong>投入已验证的渠道、地区和商品组；</li>
<li><strong>20%—30%：</strong>测试新受众、新素材及新落地页；</li>
<li><strong>10%左右：</strong>保留机动资金，用于应对素材衰退、活动节点或优质流量放量。</li>
</ul>
<p>比例不是固定答案。新品应提高探索预算，稳定SKU则应限制低毛利订单的扩量。</p>
<p>素材是否进入疲劳期，也不能只看点击率。可同步观察频次、CPM、转化率、LTV和退款率。如果频次持续上升，而转化率与净收入下滑，即使ROAS暂时稳定，也应优先轮换素材，避免把素材衰退误判为渠道失效。</p>
<h2>变化三：多账户与充值管理开始影响投放节奏</h2>
<p>预算计划执行时，常被忽略的是资金是否及时到位。独立站如果同时管理多个广告账户，应建立从“预算计划—账户余额—充值记录—平台消耗—财务对账”的完整链路。</p>
<p>建议每周固定完成以下检查：</p>
<ol>
<li>对照月度和周度预算，确认账户日均消耗上限；</li>
<li>设置余额预警，并预留节假日或大促缓冲资金；</li>
<li>检查不同账户的币种、税费、授权与付款方式；</li>
<li>将平台账单与银行或支付机构流水核对；</li>
<li>对异常消耗、重复付款及退款差异单独标记。</li>
</ol>
<p>对于需要团队协作的广告账户，可按“主账户、测试账户、地区账户”分配权限，避免多人直接修改预算和充值额度。涉及品牌词、促销词及再营销广告时，还应关注账户之间的流量重叠和转化重复归因。</p>
<h2>本周建议：先做一次预算小迁移</h2>
<p>预算优化不必一次重构全部结构。可以选择一个利润贡献较清晰、且有足够转化数据的SKU，用7—14天进行小规模测试：10%预算转向贡献毛利更高的渠道，10%转向新素材或新受众，同时保持其余条件不变。</p>
<p>复盘时不要只看点击率和单日ROAS，还要回答三个问题：净收入是否增加、退款后利润是否改善、账户余额是否支持后续放量？如果答案不清晰，就不要急于扩大预算。</p>
<blockquote>
<p>本周编辑短评：独立站投放的下一阶段，不是更会“花钱”，而是更会保留每一笔广告预算产生的利润。</p>
</blockquote>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>The most important signal this week is not a sudden surge in any single advertising channel. Cross-border DTC teams are increasingly asking a more fundamental question: <strong>Do the orders generated by advertising actually generate profit?</strong></p>
<p>Budgets have traditionally been divided by funnel stage, such as prospecting and retargeting, or by channel. That approach becomes unreliable when conversion reporting is delayed, refunds rise, or fulfillment costs fluctuate. From recent work involving ad account setup, funding, multi-account management, and financial reconciliation, the central issue has shifted from “Is the budget sufficient?” to “Where should the budget go, how much should it receive, and when should we stop spending?”</p>
<h2>Change 1: Margin Is Joining ROAS in the Budget Report</h2>
<p>Many operators still use ROAS as the primary channel metric. If 100 in advertising spend generates 300 in sales, the campaign is scaled. For DTC businesses, however, this calculation is too broad.</p>
<p>A more useful approach is:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Contribution margin = Net revenue − Cost of goods − Fulfillment and logistics − Payment fees − Refund losses − Advertising spend</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Consider an anonymous productivity app team. One channel maintained a stable ROAS, yet rising refunds caused contribution profit to decline. Rather than automatically scaling based on daily ROAS, the team calculated net profit by SKU and revised the price promise on the landing page. This was more effective than simply lowering CPC because the problem was traffic quality, not traffic price.</p>
<p>Platform-attributed revenue and actual revenue collected by the DTC store rarely match perfectly. Budget reviews should therefore distinguish among platform-attributed orders, paid orders, and net revenue after refunds. Mixing these definitions makes the budget table easier to misinterpret.</p>
<h2>Change 2: Budget Decisions Should Be Paired with Creative Fatigue Monitoring</h2>
<p>Another visible trend this week is increased acquisition-cost pressure in mature ad accounts. As a result, teams are restructuring the funds allocated to testing.</p>
<p>A practical monthly allocation model is:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>60%–70%:</strong> Proven channels, regions, and product groups;</li>
<li><strong>20%–30%:</strong> New audiences, creatives, and landing pages;</li>
<li><strong>Around 10%:</strong> Flexible funds for creative fatigue, promotional periods, or scaling opportunities.</li>
</ul>
<p>These percentages are not universal rules. New products may require more exploration budget, while stable SKUs should avoid scaling orders with weak margins.</p>
<p>Creative fatigue should not be judged by click-through rate alone. Frequency, CPM, conversion rate, LTV, and refund rate should be reviewed together. If frequency continues to rise while conversion and net revenue fall, creative fatigue may be more likely than channel failure—even if ROAS appears stable.</p>
<h2>Change 3: Funding and Account Management Now Affect Delivery Rhythm</h2>
<p>A budget plan is only useful if the funds are available when the campaign needs them. DTC teams operating multiple ad accounts should connect the entire workflow: budget plan, account balance, funding record, platform spend, and financial reconciliation.</p>
<p>A weekly operating checklist should include:</p>
<ol>
<li>Compare daily spending limits with weekly and monthly plans;</li>
<li>Set balance alerts and maintain a buffer for holidays and major promotions;</li>
<li>Verify currencies, taxes, permissions, and payment methods across accounts;</li>
<li>Match platform invoices against bank or payment-provider records;</li>
<li>Flag abnormal spend, duplicate payments, and refund discrepancies separately.</li>
</ol>
<p>Access can be organized around primary, testing, and regional accounts to prevent multiple users from modifying budgets or funding limits without coordination. Campaigns involving branded terms, promotional keywords, and retargeting also require checks for traffic overlap and duplicate conversion attribution.</p>
<h2>This Week’s Recommendation: Migrate a Small Portion of Budget</h2>
<p>There is no need to rebuild the entire budget structure at once. Select one SKU with clear profit contribution and sufficient conversion data, then run a 7–14 day test. Move 10% of the budget toward the channel with stronger contribution margin and another 10% toward a new creative or audience while keeping other variables stable.</p>
<p>The review should answer three questions: Did net revenue increase? Did profit improve after refunds? Does account funding support the next stage of scaling? If the answers remain unclear, scaling should wait.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Weekly editorial note: The next stage of DTC advertising is not about spending more intelligently in isolation. It is about preserving the profit generated by every advertising dollar.</p>
</blockquote>
